---
<Layout title="HistoryMakers Collection" description="The Toni Cook Bush Collection at The HistoryMakers Digital Library: 29 playbills, sheet music, programs, and publications tracing Will Marion Cook and Abbie Mitchell's Broadway and vaudeville years, including a complete scholarly edition of In Dahomey.">
  <div class="wrap page-title">
    <p class="eyebrow gilt">Toni Cook Bush Collection</p>
    <h1>The HistoryMakers Collection</h1>
    <p class="lede">Twenty-nine playbills, sheet music, programs, and publications, read directly off the scanned page and transcribed here — including a complete scholarly edition of <em>In Dahomey</em>.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="wrap">
    <section class="section">
      <p class="intro prose">Transcribed from the cover, or front page, of each of the 25 playbill and sheet music items in the <a href="https://dl.thehistorymakers.org/?f%5Bcollection%5D%5B%5D=Toni+Cook+Bush+Collection&view=list">Toni Cook Bush Collection</a> at The HistoryMakers Digital Library. Most of these trace Will Marion Cook's Broadway and vaudeville songwriting (<em>Bandanna Land</em>, <em>Clorindy</em>) and the 1935/1936 stage run of <em>Porgy and Bess</em>; item-level catalog metadata is minimal, so titles, credits, and publishers below come from reading the scanned page itself.</p>
      <p class="intro prose">The collection actually holds 29 items — the catalog's own search filters this page first went through only surface "Playbill" and "Sheet Music" formats, which quietly hid four more: two concert programs and two publications. One of those publications turned out to be the single most important item in the whole collection for this story: a 1996 scholarly critical edition of the complete music and scripts of <em>In Dahomey</em>, Cook's most historically significant show and the one that had been missing from every list until now. All four are below, after the playbills and sheet music.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Themes &amp; throughline</h2>
      <p class="lede">This reads less like a scrapbook and more like one family's chronicle of Black American musical theater, anchored by a single marriage: composer Will Marion Cook and singer Abbie Mitchell.</p>
      <ol class="prose themes">
        <li><strong>The Cook-Mitchell hub, 1898&ndash;1910s.</strong> Mitchell married Cook in 1898, the year he and Paul Laurence Dunbar wrote her into <em>Clorindy</em> — the show whose finale, "Darktown Is Out To-Night," is in this collection. She went on to star in nearly every Cook production held here: <em>Bandanna Land</em>, <em>The Southerners</em>, <em>The Wild Rose</em>, <em>Casino Girl</em>, and Ziegfeld's <em>Follies of 1910</em> ("Lovie Joe"). That accounts for roughly 14 of the 20 sheet music items. <em>In Dahomey</em> — arguably Cook's most historically important show, and one Mitchell also sang in — has no playbill or sheet music here, but the collection holds something better: a complete scholarly edition of its music and scripts. See below. (<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abbie_Mitchell">Wikipedia: Abbie Mitchell</a>)</li>
        <li><strong>The capstone, 1935&ndash;36 — and its coda.</strong> Mitchell's final and most celebrated stage role was Clara in the original <em>Porgy and Bess</em> — confirmed by the Forrest Theatre playbill's own cast list further down the page ("CLARA ... Abbie Mitchell"). She was the first person to record "Summertime," and this collection holds three separate <em>Porgy and Bess</em> sheet music editions plus the opening-week playbill (Jan 27, 1936). Ten weeks later, a signed recital program puts her on a stage in Washington, D.C., singing Brahms, Schubert, and Rachmaninoff — proof the momentum from Broadway carried into the concert hall.</li>
        <li><strong>The likely family link.</strong> Cook and Mitchell's son, Will Mercer Cook, became a Howard University professor and U.S. ambassador. "Cook" sitting inside "Toni Cook Bush" is very likely this same family line, which would explain why one household holds 40-plus years of one composer's catalog — this is an inference from the surname, not confirmed against her HistoryMakers biography.</li>
        <li><strong>A second, later thread: theater after Cook and Mitchell's own careers.</strong> <em>On Whitman Avenue</em> (1946, Cort Theatre) and the Theatre Union / Civic Repertory Theatre material fall outside their performing years (Cook died in 1944) but continue the collection's interest in theater that engages race and social justice. <em>On Whitman Avenue</em> is a returning-veteran housing-discrimination drama that Eleanor Roosevelt and Langston Hughes championed into a 148-performance run. (<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Civil_rights_dramas">Wikipedia: Civil rights dramas</a>)</li>
        <li><strong>A looser thread.</strong> Three items don't connect to the Cook/Mitchell story and read like general Broadway memorabilia: two <em>Little Foxes</em> items (Tallulah Bankhead's Chicago run, 1939, and a March 1941 return engagement — reviewer bylines place the 1939 item at Chicago's Selwyn Theatre, not the Broadway original), and a 1929 Curran Theatre program for Helen Hayes in <em>Coquette</em>, in San Francisco.</li>
        <li><strong>An open thread.</strong> The 1955 manuscript crediting "Lyrics by Sterling Brown / Music by Will M. Cook" postdates Cook's 1944 death by over a decade — either an unfinished Cook composition later paired with Brown's words, or a memorial/tribute piece. Unresolved.</li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="section-head"><h2>From <em>Clorindy</em> to <em>Catfish Row</em></h2></div>
      <p class="lede">Twenty-two of the collection's items, set in order — playbills, sheet music, a recital program, and one scholarly edition. Almost all trace one marriage &mdash; composer Will Marion Cook and singer Abbie Mitchell &mdash; from the cakewalk's Broadway debut in 1898 through <em>In Dahomey</em> to Mitchell's final role, as Clara in the original <em>Porgy and Bess</em>, and a recital ten weeks after.</p>

      <div class="tl-legend" role="list" aria-label="Thread key">
        <span class="tl-legend-item" role="listitem"><span class="tl-swatch" data-cat="hub"></span>Cook&ndash;Mitchell hub, 1898&ndash;1910s</span>
        <span class="tl-legend-item" role="listitem"><span class="tl-swatch" data-cat="capstone"></span><em>Porgy and Bess</em> capstone, 1935&ndash;36</span>
        <span class="tl-legend-item" role="listitem"><span class="tl-swatch" data-cat="later"></span>Later civic &amp; social-justice theater</span>
        <span class="tl-legend-item" role="listitem"><span class="tl-swatch" data-cat="other"></span>Other Broadway memorabilia</span>
      </div>

      <div class="tl-filters">
        <p class="rule-label">Trace a name through the collection</p>
        <div class="tl-chip-row" id="tlChipRow" role="group" aria-label="Filter by person">
          <button type="button" class="tl-chip" data-person="" aria-pressed="true">All <span class="tl-n">({totalEvents})</span></button>
          {timelinePeople.map((p) => (
            <button type="button" class="tl-chip" data-person={p} aria-pressed="false">{p} <span class="tl-n">({countFor(p)})</span></button>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="tl-timeline" id="tlTimeline">
        {eras.map((era) => (
          <div class="tl-era">
            <div class="tl-era-head">
              <p class="tl-era-label">{era.label}</p>
              <h3>{era.title}</h3>
            </div>
            {era.events.map((ev) => (
              <article class="tl-event" data-cat={ev.cat} data-people={ev.people.join('|')}>
                <div class="tl-event-top">
                  <h4>{ev.title}</h4>
                  <span class="tl-event-date">{ev.date}</span>
                </div>
                {ev.note && <p class="tl-event-note">{ev.note}</p>}
                {ev.people.length > 0 && (
                  <div class="tl-people">
                    {ev.people.map((p) => (
                      timelinePeople.includes(p)
                        ? <button type="button" class="tl-tag" data-person={p}>{p}</button>
                        : <span class="tl-tag tl-tag-static">{p}</span>
                    ))}
                  </div>
                )}
                <div class="tl-items">
                  {ev.items.map((it) => (
                    <div class="tl-item-row">
                      <a href={it.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="tl-item-thumb">
                        <Image src={imageFor(it.url)} alt={it.title} width={56} />
                      </a>
                      <span class="tl-it-title">{it.title}</span>
                      <a href={it.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="tl-item-link">View item &rarr;</a>
                    </div>
                  ))}
                </div>
              </article>
            ))}
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>

      <p class="footnote">Dates not printed on the item itself are dated to the Broadway production they name, via Playbill Vault, IBDB, or Wikipedia, and marked "undated" where no production could be confirmed.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="section-head"><h2>Playbills <span class="count tabular">5</span></h2></div>
      <div class="hm-grid">
        {playbills.map((row) => {
          const pages = pagesFor(row.href);
          return (
            <article class="hm-card">
              <a href={row.href} target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="hm-card-image">
                <Image src={row.image} alt={row.production} width={480} widths={[240, 480, 720]} sizes="(max-width: 640px) 88vw, (max-width: 1000px) 44vw, 300px" />
              </a>
              <div class="hm-card-body">
                <h3>{row.production}</h3>
                <p class="hm-card-credits">{row.credits}</p>
                <p class="hm-card-notes">{row.notes}</p>
                <div class="btn-row">
                  <a class="btn" href={row.href} target="_blank" rel="noopener">View at HistoryMakers &rarr;</a>
                </div>
                {pages && pages.length > 1 && (
                  <div class="hm-pages" data-pages>
                    <button type="button" class="hm-pages-toggle" data-pages-toggle>View all {pages.length} pages &rarr;</button>
                    <div class="hm-viewer" data-viewer hidden>
                      <div class="hm-viewer-stage">
                        <button type="button" class="hm-viewer-nav prev" data-prev aria-label="Previous page">&lsaquo;</button>
                        <div class="hm-viewer-frame" data-frame>
                          {pages.map((src, i) => (
                            <img src={src} alt={`${row.production}, page ${i + 1}`} class={i === 0 ? 'current' : undefined} loading="lazy" />
                          ))}
                        </div>
                        <button type="button" class="hm-viewer-nav next" data-next aria-label="Next page">&rsaquo;</button>
                      </div>
                      <p class="hm-viewer-counter"><span data-counter>1</span> / {pages.length}</p>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                )}
              </div>
            </article>
          );
        })}
      </div>
      <p class="footnote">Playbills are digitized as multi-page booklets (4 to 35 pages each); each card above reflects only what is printed on the front cover, or for the Porgy and Bess Forrest Theatre program, the first inside page, which carries the full cast list.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="section-head"><h2>Sheet music <span class="count tabular">20</span></h2></div>
      <div class="hm-grid">
        {sheetMusic.map((row) => {
          const pages = pagesFor(row.href);
          return (
            <article class="hm-card">
              <a href={row.href} target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="hm-card-image">
                <Image src={row.image} alt={row.title} width={480} widths={[240, 480, 720]} sizes="(max-width: 640px) 88vw, (max-width: 1000px) 44vw, 300px" />
              </a>
              <div class="hm-card-body">
                <h3>{row.title}</h3>
                <p class="hm-card-credits">{row.credits}</p>
                <p class="hm-card-notes">{row.notes}</p>
                <div class="btn-row">
                  <a class="btn" href={row.href} target="_blank" rel="noopener">View at HistoryMakers &rarr;</a>
                </div>
                {pages && pages.length > 1 && (
                  <div class="hm-pages" data-pages>
                    <button type="button" class="hm-pages-toggle" data-pages-toggle>View all {pages.length} pages &rarr;</button>
                    <div class="hm-viewer" data-viewer hidden>
                      <div class="hm-viewer-stage">
                        <button type="button" class="hm-viewer-nav prev" data-prev aria-label="Previous page">&lsaquo;</button>
                        <div class="hm-viewer-frame" data-frame>
                          {pages.map((src, i) => (
                            <img src={src} alt={`${row.title}, page ${i + 1}`} class={i === 0 ? 'current' : undefined} loading="lazy" />
                          ))}
                        </div>
                        <button type="button" class="hm-viewer-nav next" data-next aria-label="Next page">&rsaquo;</button>
                      </div>
                      <p class="hm-viewer-counter"><span data-counter>1</span> / {pages.length}</p>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                )}
              </div>
            </article>
          );
        })}
      </div>
      <p class="footnote">Titles, credits, and wording above are transcribed as printed on each cover, including period-typical language on some early-1900s covers.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="section-head"><h2>Beyond the playbills and sheet music <span class="count tabular">3</span></h2></div>
      <p class="lede">Filed under "Programs/Events" and "Publication" — formats easy to miss if you only search for playbills and sheet music. The first is the one that matters most.</p>
      <div class="hm-grid">
        {beyond.map((row) => {
          const pages = pagesFor(row.href);
          return (
            <article class="hm-card">
              <a href={row.href} target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="hm-card-image">
                <Image src={row.image} alt={row.title} width={480} widths={[240, 480, 720]} sizes="(max-width: 640px) 88vw, (max-width: 1000px) 44vw, 300px" />
              </a>
              <div class="hm-card-body">
                <h3>{row.title}</h3>
                <p class="hm-card-credits">{row.credits}</p>
                <p class="hm-card-notes">{row.notes}</p>
                <div class="btn-row">
                  <a class="btn" href={row.href} target="_blank" rel="noopener">View at HistoryMakers &rarr;</a>
                </div>
                {pages && pages.length > 1 && (
                  <div class="hm-pages" data-pages>
                    <button type="button" class="hm-pages-toggle" data-pages-toggle>View all {pages.length} pages &rarr;</button>
                    <div class="hm-viewer" data-viewer hidden>
                      <div class="hm-viewer-stage">
                        <button type="button" class="hm-viewer-nav prev" data-prev aria-label="Previous page">&lsaquo;</button>
                        <div class="hm-viewer-frame" data-frame>
                          {pages.map((src, i) => (
                            <img src={src} alt={`${row.title}, page ${i + 1}`} class={i === 0 ? 'current' : undefined} loading="lazy" />
                          ))}
                        </div>
                        <button type="button" class="hm-viewer-nav next" data-next aria-label="Next page">&rsaquo;</button>
                      </div>
                      <p class="hm-viewer-counter"><span data-counter>1</span> / {pages.length}</p>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                )}
              </div>
            </article>
          );
        })}
      </div>
      <p class="footnote">The In Dahomey volume runs 317 pages, so it's cover-only here; the other two page through in full. A fourth item this search also surfaced, a 181-page family genealogy volume ("Bonds Conway, 1763-1843: Ancestors and Descendants"), isn't part of the Cook-Mitchell story and isn't shown here.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Notes</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>Two pairs of items are duplicate scans: the two handwritten "Sterling Brown / Will M. Cook" manuscript pages are visually identical, and the two "Wid de Moon, Moon, Moon" covers are the same edition. Both are listed above as separate catalog entries since they are separate items in the collection.</p>
        <p>Most sheet music in this collection centers on composer Will Marion Cook and his collaborators (Paul Laurence Dunbar, James Weldon Johnson, Alex Rogers), spanning roughly 1898 (<em>Clorindy</em>) to the 1900s Broadway/vaudeville era, plus a cluster of George Gershwin <em>Porgy and Bess</em> material from the 1930s and a 1955-dated Sterling Brown manuscript note — suggesting the collection's Cook-era pieces are historical/archival items rather than Toni Cook Bush's own performance repertoire, while the <em>Porgy and Bess</em> and 1955 items may relate more directly to her.</p>
        <p>Catalog metadata (creator, format, page count) is uniform and minimal across all 25 items; everything above was read directly off each scanned cover page rather than pulled from a metadata field.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Source</h2>
      <p class="prose">The full collection, with scans of every page, is at The HistoryMakers Digital Library: <a href="https://dl.thehistorymakers.org/?f%5Bcollection%5D%5B%5D=Toni+Cook+Bush+Collection&view=list">Toni Cook Bush Collection</a>.</p>
      <div class="btn-row" style="margin-top:12px"><a class="btn" href={`${base}/timeline`}>See the site timeline</a><a class="btn" href={`${base}/archive`}>See the archive</a></div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layout>

<script>
  const chipRow = document.getElementById('tlChipRow');
  const timeline = document.getElementById('tlTimeline');
  if (chipRow && timeline) {
    let active: string | null = null;

    const applyFilter = () => {
      chipRow.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('.tl-chip').forEach((chip) => {
        chip.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(chip.dataset.person === (active ?? '')));
      });
      timeline.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('.tl-event').forEach((card) => {
        const people = (card.dataset.people || '').split('|').filter(Boolean);
        card.classList.toggle('dim', Boolean(active) && !people.includes(active as string));
      });
    };

    const setActive = (person: string) => {
      active = active === person ? null : person || null;
      applyFilter();
    };

    chipRow.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
      const chip = (e.target as HTMLElement).closest<HTMLButtonElement>('.tl-chip');
      if (chip) setActive(chip.dataset.person || '');
    });

    timeline.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
      const tag = (e.target as HTMLElement).closest<HTMLButtonElement>('.tl-tag:not(.tl-tag-static)');
      if (tag) setActive(tag.dataset.person || '');
    });
  }
</script>

<script>
  document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-pages]').forEach((root) => {
    const toggle = root.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-pages-toggle]');
    const viewer = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-viewer]');
    const frame = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-frame]');
    const prev = root.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-prev]');
    const next = root.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-next]');
    const counter = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-counter]');
    if (!toggle || !viewer || !frame || !prev || !next || !counter) return;
    const imgs = Array.from(frame.querySelectorAll<HTMLImageElement>('img'));
    const total = imgs.length;
    const openLabel = `View all ${total} pages →`;
    let page = 1;

    const show = (n: number) => {
      page = Math.max(1, Math.min(total, n));
      imgs.forEach((img, i) => img.classList.toggle('current', i + 1 === page));
      counter.textContent = String(page);
      prev.disabled = page === 1;
      next.disabled = page === total;
      const cur = imgs[page - 1];
      if (cur.loading === 'lazy') cur.loading = 'eager';
      const nxt = imgs[page];
      if (nxt && nxt.loading === 'lazy') nxt.loading = 'eager';
    };

    toggle.addEventListener('click', () => {
      const opening = viewer.hidden;
      viewer.hidden = !opening ? true : false;
      toggle.textContent = opening ? 'Hide pages' : openLabel;
      if (opening) show(1);
    });
    prev.addEventListener('click', () => show(page - 1));
    next.addEventListener('click', () => show(page + 1));
    show(1);
  });
</script>

<style>
  .intro { color: var(--ink-2); font-size: 16.5px; }
